NOTE: The above code includes asserts for the count property of ArrayList and the Contains method. For other methods, the user needs to create appropriate assert conditions based on the method's function.--------------------Reading and outputting assert statements from C# code would require some preprocessing to make the comment-lines into actual assert statements. Here's a generic Python script to do this:

```python
import re

def convert_csharp_code_to_assert(code):
    lines = code.split('\n')
    assert_lines = [line for line in lines if line.strip().startswith("// @@@")]
    for line in assert_lines:
        pattern = re.compile('// @@@ (.*)')
        comment = re.search(pattern, line)
        if comment:
            print('Debug.Assert({});'.format(comment.group(1)))

# Sample usage:
csharp_code = """
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    sum = x + y;
    // @@@ sum is greater than or equal to x and y
    return sum;
}
"""

convert_csharp_code_to_assert(csharp_code)
```

This script will read each line of the code and check if it is a comment that starts with '// @@@'. If it is, it will print out a Debug.Assert() statement. Be sure to use the right code for your specific situation.--------------------Here is the script that solves this problem:

```python
import re

def convert_to_assert(code):
    # Search for comments starting with "@@@"
    comments = re.findall(r'/\*@@@(.*?)\*/', code, re.DOTALL)
    for comment in comments:
        # Find the comment description and replace it with the assert statement
        comment_description = comment.strip()
        new_code = re.sub('\/\*@@@?\s?'+comment_description+'\s?\*\/', 'Debug.Assert('+comment_description+');', code)
        code = new_code
    return code


code = '''
// ... (C# code here)
'''

print(convert_to_assert(code))

``` 
In the above script, 're' is a python built-in module that provides support for regular expressions. It lets you find patterns in string.

Please note that above code is basic - it will convert each "@@@" comment to debug.assert, but it would not cover every possible condition or every comment. You would need to improve it or add special handling to meet your specific requirements.--------------------Here is a Python-based solution for the task which makes use of the "ast" and "re" libraries

```python
import ast
import re

def extract_comments(node):
    if isinstance(node, ast.Expr):
        comment = node.value
        if isinstance(comment, (ast.Str, ast.Bytes)):
            text = comment.s.strip()
            if re.match(r"^\s*//@@@", text):
                yield text
        yield from extract_comments(node.value)
    elif isinstance(node, ast.AugAssign):
        yield from extract_comments(node.targets)
        yield from extract_comments(node.value)
    elif isinstance(node, list):
        for item in node:
            yield from extract_comments(item)
    else:
        for field, value in ast.iter_fields(node):
            yield from extract_comments(value)

def generate_assert_statements(code):
    tree = ast.parse(code)
    for comment in extract_comments(tree):
        assert_statement = comment.replace("//@@@", "Debug.Assert")
        print(f"<code>{assert_statement}</code>")

code = """
//...
"""

generate_assert_statements(code)
```
It's important to note that this code will replace "//@@@" comments with "Debug.Assert".
Also, this is a simplistic example. In real-world scenarios, you may want to handle multiple types of comments, nesting, etc. 
